---
// Triggers vs Collisions — the physics-family reference (Unit 2, used all year).
import Base from '../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const crumbs = `<nav class="crumbs"><a href="../index.html">Docs</a> &gt; <a href="../unit-02/index.html">Unit 2</a> &gt; <span>Triggers vs Collisions</span></nav>`;

const body = `<h1>Triggers vs Collisions</h1>

<p class="lesson-lead">
    Unity physics gives you two ways for two objects to meet, and every
    interactive thing you build this year is one or the other. Pick wrong
    and your coin pushes the player around the screen; pick right and the
    whole game feels invisible — which is the point. This is the complete
    reference for the physics family: pickups, hazards, hitboxes, ladders.
</p>

<h2>The one-line rule</h2>
<p>
    <strong>A trigger asks "did we overlap?" A collider asks "can we
    occupy the same space?"</strong> — triggers are for <em>events</em>
    (you entered a region); collisions are for <em>physics</em> (you
    cannot walk through a wall). Everything else follows from that.
</p>

<table>
    <tr><th></th><th>Trigger</th><th>Collider (non-trigger)</th></tr>
    <tr><td>Is Trigger checked?</td><td>Yes</td><td>No</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Does physics resolve the overlap?</td><td>No — objects pass through</td><td>Yes — objects push/stop</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Message you receive</td><td>OnTriggerEnter2D / Stay2D / Exit2D</td><td>OnCollisionEnter2D / Stay2D / Exit2D</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Handler argument</td><td>Collider2D (no contact points)</td><td>Collision2D (with contact points)</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Needs a Rigidbody?</td><td>At least one object in the pair</td><td>At least one object in the pair</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Your game uses it for</td><td>coins, potions, ladders, hitboxes, prompts</td><td>ground, walls, spikes, enemy contact</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>Enter, Stay, Exit — the three moments</h2>
<p>
    Every overlap has a lifecycle, and Unity calls one method per moment:
</p>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Enter</strong> fires ONCE, the first frame the shapes overlap</li>
    <li><strong>Stay</strong> fires EVERY frame the overlap continues</li>
    <li><strong>Exit</strong> fires ONCE, the first frame they no longer overlap</li>
</ul>
<p>
    The classic bug pair: <strong>Enter-only</strong> handlers mean "one
    event per bump" (a coin is collected once — but standing inside a
    hazard deals damage only once). <strong>Stay</strong> handlers mean
    "an event every frame" (a continuous hazard — but a coin collected
    in Stay would try to collect itself 60 times a second and crash on
    the second, because Destroy already removed it).
</p>
<p>
    You will meet a hazard that genuinely needs <em>both</em> in
    <a href="../../lessons/4.4.html">4.4</a>, when the enemy attacks you.
    Damage on contact handles the first bump; damage while you remain in
    contact is what stops you simply standing inside an enemy forever.
    The moment you use Stay, you owe it a <strong>cooldown</strong> —
    because Stay fires every frame, and without a cooldown it drains your
    whole health bar in about a twentieth of a second.
</p>

<h2>The tag check — the first branch of every handler</h2>
<p>
    A handler fires for EVERY overlapping object — walls, other enemies,
    the player, your own projectiles. The first line of almost every
    handler you write this year filters with CompareTag:
</p>
<p>
    Every handler you write this year has the same three-part shape:
    <strong>filter</strong> (which object did we touch?), <strong>guard</strong>
    (is everything I need actually there?), then <strong>act and clean up</strong>
    (change the number, remove the pickup). You will write that shape four
    times before the year is out — coin, potion, axe, and damage — and the
    <em>only</em> parts that change are the tag you filter on and the number
    you change.
</p>
<p>
    The silent-failure trap: a misspelled tag does not throw. The branch
    simply never matches and nothing happens, with no error to follow. When a
    trigger "does nothing," check the tag's spelling FIRST — in both places:
    the object's Tag field, and the string in your code.
</p>

<h2>Why pickups are triggers and hazards are colliders</h2>
<p>
    A coin must not shove the player when collected — overlap is an
    event, not a wall: trigger. A spike is a physical thing you are
    stopped by or standing on, AND an event when touched — collider,
    with the damage handler riding on the collision message. The
    turret's projectile splits the difference, and you will decide this
    yourself in <a href="../../lessons/3.4.html">3.4</a>: its collider is
    usually a <strong>trigger</strong> (a bullet passes through the player's
    body until it chooses to "hit"), but it dies on contact with whatever you
    decide should stop it. Note that a bullet also needs a
    <strong>Rigidbody2D</strong> before any of this fires — one of the two
    objects in the pair must have a body, exactly as with the coin.
</p>

<h2>The settings that break everything</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Is Trigger unchecked when you meant it checked:</strong>
        your pickup becomes a wall. Symptom: the player stops short of
        the coin instead of collecting it.</li>
    <li><strong>Rigidbody-less pairs:</strong> two static colliders
        never generate trigger or collision messages — at least one
        object in any pair needs a Rigidbody2D (the player carries
        one; enemies too). For a <em>collision</em> message it has to be
        a <strong>Dynamic</strong> body: a Kinematic body produces no
        collision messages against static colliders, so it passes
        through a wall in silence. Triggers are the exception — a
        Kinematic body still fires those.</li>
    <li><strong>Layer collisions off:</strong> the Physics 2D matrix
        (Project Settings) can disable interactions between whole
        LAYERS — if your layer pair is off there, no message ever
        fires, tags or not. This is the same layer machinery as the
        ground-check raycast mask
        (<a href="../../lessons/6.2.html">6.2</a>).</li>
    <li><strong>Z position / sorting:</strong> sorting layers decide
        what DRAWS in front; physics happens regardless. An object
        drawn behind a wall still collides with it — visual depth and
        physical space are separate systems
        (<a href="../../lessons/7.2.html">7.2</a>).</li>
</ul>

<h2>Where you meet each pattern this year</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Trigger + tag + singleton:</strong> coins and potions
        (<a href="../../lessons/2.1.html">2.1</a>,
        <a href="../../lessons/2.3.html">2.3</a>), the axe
        (<a href="../../lessons/5.1.html">5.1</a>), the boots
        (<a href="../../lessons/6.4.html">6.4</a>), the ladder zone
        (<a href="../../lessons/7.3.html">7.3</a>)</li>
    <li><strong>Collision + damage + cooldown:</strong> spikes
        (<a href="../../lessons/2.2.html">2.2</a>), enemy contact
        (<a href="../../lessons/4.4.html">4.4</a>)</li>
    <li><strong>Trigger hitboxes:</strong> your melee swing
        (<a href="../../lessons/8.3.html">8.3</a>), the boss's
        shockwave (<a href="../../lessons/8.4.html">8.4</a>)</li>
</ul>

<div class="unit-nav">
    <span><a href="../unit-02/index.html">← Unit 2</a></span>
    <span><a href="../index.html">All docs</a></span>
    <span><a href="../reference/unity-csharp-quickref.html">C# Quick Reference →</a></span>
</div>`;
